WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.660
Token is a broad word.

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:21.720
A session identifier, opaque access token, JWT, refresh token, API key, reset token, invite token, CSRF token, OAuth state value, one-time code, webhook signing secret, and CI/CD credential all solve different problems.

3
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.660
Treating them as one category encourages vague designs.

4
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.440
The first review question should be: what is this token for?

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:42.360
A token may identify a user, authenticate a client, authorize a workflow, bind a browser request, verify message integrity, grant temporary access, or let a service act on another system.

6
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.140
Those purposes require different authority limits.

7
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:55.760
Audience, subject, scope, purpose, tenant, environment, expiration, and context should be explicit where appropriate.

8
00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:01.460
A token intended for one system should not be accepted by another just because it has a familiar shape.

9
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.420
Public identifiers and bearer secrets also need different designs.

10
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.560
A correlation ID may be public and useful for tracing.

11
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.540
A password reset token is a bearer secret that can change account state.

12
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.400
An API key may grant durable access to production data.

13
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.860
A webhook signing secret does not identify a user but protects message integrity.

14
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.920
The controls depend on what the token can do if copied.

15
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:41.160
Token flows cross many trust boundaries: browsers, mobile apps, APIs, identity providers, databases, caches, reverse proxies, logs, CI/CD systems, serverless platforms, and third-party services.

16
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:51.820
A useful design states who issues the token, who validates it, where it is stored, where it is transmitted, what authority it carries, and what happens when it is exposed.

17
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.600
Without that map, token handling becomes guesswork.
